cmn2wa9cn0097jc3oydcznjr2	gen-permissions-reference	general	Roles & Permissions	both	{}	How do permissions work in the application?	How do permissions work in the application?\nPermission system explained\nHow is access controlled?\nWhat is resource action pattern?\nPermission scopes explained\nWhy can't I see a page?\nWhy is a button disabled?\nHow are menu items filtered?\nWhy don't I have access?\nPermission enforcement\nPermissions control what you can see and do. They use a resource:action pattern with three scope levels. Permissions are enforced at both the UI level (hiding options) and the API level (blocking requests).\n**Resource:Action pattern** — Each permission targets a specific resource (e.g., compliance.controls) and action (e.g., create). Example: `compliance.controls:create` means the ability to create compliance controls.\n**Actions available**: **view** (read-only), **create** (add new records), **edit** (modify existing), **delete** (remove records), **approve** (approve/reject submissions).\n**Three permission scopes**: **Global** (all records across the organization), **Department** (only records within the user's assigned department), **Own** (only records owned by or assigned to the user).\n**UI enforcement** — If you lack permission for a page, its sidebar menu item is hidden. If you lack permission for an action, the button (Create, Edit, Delete) is hidden or disabled.\n**API enforcement** — Even if you manually navigate to a URL, the server will reject unauthorized requests. This ensures security regardless of how the application is accessed.\n**Role determines permissions** — Your assigned role maps to a predefined set of resource:action:scope permissions. You cannot customize permissions beyond your role's definition.\nIf a sidebar menu item or action button is not visible, it means your role does not have the required permission — this is by design, not a bug\nPermissions cannot be changed on a per-user basis — they are tied to roles. C